{
  "title": "PyHealth \u2014 Healthcare & Clinical AI (1,591\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: PyHealth\nDomain: Healthcare & Clinical AI | Scale: medium | Stars: 1,591\nProblem: PyHealth simplifies clinical predictive modeling and healthcare AI application development by providing a comprehensive deep learning toolkit with pre-built models, healthcare-specific data processing, and standardized pipelines for both ML researchers and medical practitioners.\nTarget users: Machine learning researchers, Medical practitioners, Healthcare AI developers, Clinical data scientists\nCore features: Modular 5-stage pipeline for healthcare ML workflows; 33+ pre-built deep learning models with production-ready trainer/metrics; Support for 10+ healthcare tasks and clinical datasets (MIMIC, eICU, OMOP); Medical code and electronic health record (EHR) processing; Fast data processing (~3x faster than pandas) for quick experimentation; Benchmarking and planned paper implementations\nIntegrations: MIMIC clinical dataset, eICU dataset, OMOP data standard, Hugging Face Transformers, PyTorch ecosystem, RDKit for molecular data\nTech stack: Python 3.12+ + Docker, PyPI distribution\nKey libraries: PyTorch 2.7.1, Transformers 4.53.2, scikit-learn 1.7.0, Polars 1.35.2, Pandas 2.3.1, Dask 2025.11.0, Pydantic 2.11.7, PyArrow 22.0.0, MNE 1.10.0, NetworkX, einops, linear-attention-transformer\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Healthcare-first design: specialized support for medical codes, EHR standards (MIMIC, eICU, OMOP), and clinical data pipelines rather than generic ML toolkit\n- Modular pipeline abstraction: 5-stage pipeline design enables researchers and practitioners to customize workflows while maintaining standardization across projects\n- Performance optimization: deliberate use of Polars, Dask, and PyArrow for 3x faster data processing over pandas, addressing real pain points in clinical dataset preprocessing\n- Production-ready components: emphasis on trainer/metrics/model maturity and benchmarking against published papers indicates focus on reproducibility and deployment readiness in clinical settings",
